INT. SEAFOOD MARKET - BACK OFFICE - PRE-DAWN

A tiled room that smells of ice and brine. A desk with a ledger and a crushed pack of cigarettes. Through a window, the market floor is dark, tubs of crab stacked like tombstones.

VITO FERRANTINO (60s, apron over a good shirt) sits behind the desk with his hands folded. Across from him, his nephew DANTE FERRANTINO (30s, leather coat, wet hair) shifts in a metal chair.

By the door stands MRS. ODETTE LAROCHE (50s, orange rubber gloves to the elbow, fishmonger's cap), eating an apple. And in the corner, on an overturned crate, sits SUNDAR IYER (20s, soft-spoken, clean, holding a clipboard).

VITO
(to the room)
Everybody comfortable?

Nobody answers. Odette bites the apple. Dante's knee bounces.

VITO (CONT'D)
(to Dante)
You want some coffee?

DANTE
I'm good.

VITO
It's early. You look tired.

DANTE
I'm not tired.

VITO
(to himself)
He's not tired.

He opens the ledger. Turns a page. Runs a finger down a column.

VITO (CONT'D)
(to Sundar)
Accountant. How's the arithmetic?

SUNDAR
It's close, Mr. Ferrantino.

VITO
Close to what?

SUNDAR
It's close to correct.

VITO
Close. Odette?

ODETTE
Close is a nice word.

VITO
It's a nice word until the fish go off.

Dante laughs once. Short. Dry. Nobody joins.

VITO (CONT'D)
(to Dante)
You think it's funny?

DANTE
You said fish.

VITO
I did say fish.

He closes the ledger. Slides it across the desk. Dante glances at it. Doesn't touch it.

VITO (CONT'D)
Read it.

DANTE
Uncle Vito.

VITO
Read the third page.

DANTE
I know what's on the third page.

ODETTE
Then why are you sweating?

SUNDAR
Dante, I told you to take it back.

DANTE
You told me nothing!

Both speak at once, sharp and loud.

ODETTE
(to Sundar)
Hush.

^SUNDAR
(to Odette)
I didn't say--

VITO
(to the room, calm)
Enough.

Silence. The ice machine rumbles in the next room. Vito lifts the lid of the ledger one more time and takes out a single photograph. He sets it face-up on the desk.

It's Dante, in a car, handing an envelope to a stranger in a dark coat. The timestamp reads last Thursday.

DANTE
(barely)
Where did you get that?

VITO
The man in the coat is mine. Has been for ten years. He told me you'd come.

Dante looks at Odette. At Sundar. At the door. Odette drops the apple core into the bin and folds her gloved arms.

VITO (CONT'D)
(quietly, to himself)
Blood.

He lifts the phone on the desk and dials one digit. It rings once.

FADE OUT.
